<div class="wrap prose"><p class="muted">Nov 25, 2026 · Data from the Nov 25 nightly run, compared with Nov 18 · refreshed from nightly reports</p>
<h1 class="ph">Why npm MCP servers fail more than plugins (Nov 25 mid-week check)</h1>
<p class="answer">As of Nov 25, 2026, 921 of 1,085 sandboxed installs of skills, plugins and MCP servers passed in Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Cursor and Pi (86.9% of the 1,060 installs we can judge without an API key). A week earlier (Nov 18) it was 912 of 1,075 (86.9%). While marketplace plugins reliably clear 98% in Claude Code and Grok Build, community npm MCP packages pass only 42.2%–48.9% across the five agents because many require credentials, positional CLI arguments or extra local services.</p>

<h2>How did each agent do this week?</h2>
<p class="answer">Across the five CLIs on Nov 25, agents that test marketplace plugins show higher raw pass rates than agents tested mainly or solely on MCP servers and GitHub packages.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Passed (Nov 25)</th><th>Rate</th><th>Nov 18</th><th>Change (points)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/works-in/claude-code/">Claude Code</a></th><td>345 / 369</td><td>93.5%</td><td>93.2%</td><td>+0.3</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/works-in/grok-build/">Grok Build</a></th><td>358 / 384</td><td>93.2%</td><td>92.7%</td><td>+0.5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/works-in/codex/">Codex</a></th><td>97 / 117</td><td>82.9%</td><td>83.5%</td><td>-0.6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/works-in/cursor/">Cursor</a></th><td>34 / 52</td><td>65.4%</td><td>66.0%</td><td>-0.6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/works-in/pi/">Pi</a></th><td>87 / 138</td><td>63.0%</td><td>64.0%</td><td>-1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Each agent is tested only on the add-on formats its CLI supports, so this table measures ecosystem readiness rather than ranking the agents themselves. Cursor only runs MCP servers and standalone skills (no plugin installer), which pulls its raw pass rate down whenever community npm MCP servers require setup.</p>

<h2>Why do npm MCP servers fail more often than plugins?</h2>
<p class="answer">Community npm MCP packages have to start a live server process and complete an MCP handshake inside a clean, keyless Linux sandbox, whereas plugins only need to unpack files and register their metadata.</p>
<div class="tw"><table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Add-ons</th><th>Installs</th><th>Passed</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Plugins</th><td>373</td><td>800</td><td>761</td><td>95.1%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">MCP servers (npm)</th><td>45</td><td>225</td><td>102</td><td>45.3%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">MCP servers</th><td>9</td><td>45</td><td>43</td><td>95.6%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Agent Skills</th><td>3</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>100.0%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>In our Nov 20, 2026 baseline (<code>testing/results/2026-11-20.json</code>), official reference and curated MCP servers passed 8–9 of 9 per agent (88.9%–100%), while the 45 npm MCP packages passed 16–23 of 45 (35.6%–51.1%). Official plugins hit 98.1% in Claude Code (311/317), 98.2% in Grok Build (326/332), and 100% in Codex (65/65). Remember that our harness pre-fetches npm packages into a shared cache before timing each agent's connect check, so an npm MCP failure here is not a cold download timeout; it means the server refused to connect without a token, path argument or local daemon.</p>

<h2>Which MCP servers pass across all five agents?</h2>
<p class="answer">On Nov 25, 26 of 54 tested MCP servers connected cleanly in all five agents (Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Cursor and Pi) with zero credentials configured.</p>
